<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.1.4: Creating Expressions to Estimate Cost, Part 2</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00058</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.1.4: Creating Expressions to Estimate Cost, Part 2</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cb92495e-810a-4f69-af9f-ea431b0df951</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">At the end of the school year, all students in the ninth grade are invited to an ice cream party. Use the skills you learned from planning a pizza party to answer the following:</para>
<!--Q1-->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00002">Identify and describe at least one letter that you will use to represent the cost of something you need to buy for the party. </para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00003">Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00005">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00001">
      <item>\(t\) = number of different toppings one student selects</item>
      <item>\(s\) = number of different scoops of ice cream one student requests</item>
      <item> \(n\) = total number of ninth grade students at party </item>
      <item>\(p\) = cost of paper products like napkins, bowls, and spoons</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Q2-->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00006">Write an expression that could represent an estimated cost for the party using that letter. Note, you may use other letters, too, if needed. </para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    <para id="para-00007">Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00009">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00002">
      <item>\((0.75s + 1.5t)n =\) total cost</item>
      <item>\(4.00n + 15 =\) total cost</item>
      <item>\(1.50n+0.08(1.50n) =\) total cost</item>
      <item>\(\frac{125}{6} \cdot 5 + 6(3) + p =\) total cost</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Q3-->
<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00010">Describe your expression in words. </para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    <para id="para-00011">Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00013">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00003">
      <item>Each scoop of ice cream costs $0.75 and the cost of each topping is $1.50 which sum to the cost of one bowl of ice cream. Now we multiply the cost of one bowl of ice cream by the number of students in the class. </item>
      <item>A flat cost of $4 per bowl of ice cream for each student plus a charge of $15 for the bowls, spoons, and napkins.</item>
      <item>There are \(n\) students in the ninth grade. A packaged ice cream cone is about $1.50, and tax is about 8%.</item>
      <item>There are 125 students in the ninth grade. Suppose a tub of ice cream costs $5 and is enough for 6 students. A pack of 24 cones costs about $3, and 6 packs are needed. A pack of 150 paper napkins costs \(p\) dollars.</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Q4-->
<exercise id="exer-00004">
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    <para id="para-00014">Using the letter you identified in question 1, describe the values that would be reasonable for the quantity that the letter represents. </para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
    
    <para id="para-00015">Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00017">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00004">
      <item>You may choose to limit each student to selecting at most five toppings, \(t\), per bowl of ice cream. Or, \(t \leq 5\).</item>
      <item>The number of scoops of ice cream, \(s\), provided to each student should not exceed three. Or, \(s \leq 3\)</item>
      <item>The number of students, \(n\), at the ninth grade party will probably be less than 730. Or,\( n \lt 730\). </item>
      <item>A whole number between 90 and 100 is a reasonable value for the number of students in ninth grade, or 90 \(\leq n \geq 100\).</item>
      <item>A number less than $4 but more than $1 is reasonable for the cost of a pack of paper napkins, or \(1 \lt n \lt\) 4.</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content>
